[Thinking]
**Creating a search input with button**

The user wants a code snippet for an input field with a search icon on the right, which should be clickable and call a function once the input has a value. I need to provide examples in HTML/CSS/JS and maybe a React version too. An accessible button using SVG should be included, as well as an event handler that calls the function with the input's value.

I'll focus on keeping everything concise and clear.
[/Thinking]

Below are two minimal examples you can pick from — a plain HTML/JS version and a React version. Both place an SVG “reading glass” (magnifying-glass) icon on the right side of the input, make it keyboard-accessible/clickable, and call a function with the current input value when clicked or when Enter is pressed.

1) Plain HTML + CSS + JS
- Uses a wrapper with relative positioning.
- The icon is a button (accessible, focusable) positioned absolutely on the right.
- The JS reads the input value and calls searchHandler(value).

HTML:
```html
<div class="search-wrap">
  <input id="searchInput" type="text" placeholder="Search..." />
  <button id="searchBtn" class="search-btn" aria-label="Search">
    <!-- simple magnifying glass SVG -->
    <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
      <path d="M11 4a7 7 0 1 0 0 14 7 7 0 0 0 0-14z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
      <path d="M21 21l-4.35-4.35" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
    </svg>
  </button>
</div>
```

CSS:
```css
.search-wrap {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-block;
}

.search-wrap input {
  padding-right: 40px; /* room for the icon */
  height: 36px;
  font-size: 14px;
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

.search-btn {
  position: absolute;
  right: 6px;
  top: 50%;
  transform: translateY(-50%);
  border: none;
  background: transparent;
  padding: 4px;
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: #333;
}

/* optional: focus/hover styles */
.search-btn:focus,
.search-btn:hover {
  color: #000;
  outline: none;
}
```

JavaScript:
```javascript
const input = document.getElementById('searchInput');
const btn = document.getElementById('searchBtn');

function searchHandler(query) {
  // your function: do anything with query
  console.log('Search called with:', query);
}

btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
  const value = input.value.trim();
  if (value !== '') searchHandler(value);
});

// optional: allow Enter to trigger search when input focused
input.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
  if (e.key === 'Enter') {
    const value = input.value.trim();
    if (value !== '') searchHandler(value);
  }
});
```

2) React (functional component)
- Uses a button for the icon and calls onSearch prop with current value.
- Keeps input state with useState.

JSX + CSS (same styling approach as above):
```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

export default function SearchInput({ onSearch }) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState('');

  function handleSearch() {
    const v = value.trim();
    if (v !== '') onSearch?.(v);
  }

  function onKeyDown(e) {
    if (e.key === 'Enter') handleSearch();
  }

  return (
    <div className="search-wrap" style={{ position: 'relative', display: 'inline-block' }}>
      <input
        value={value}
        onChange={(e) => setValue(e.target.value)}
        onKeyDown={onKeyDown}
        placeholder="Search..."
        style={{ paddingRight: 40, height: 36, fontSize: 14 }}
      />
      <button
        onClick={handleSearch}
        aria-label="Search"
        style={{
          position: 'absolute',
          right: 6,
          top: '50%',
          transform: 'translateY(-50%)',
          border: 'none',
          background: 'transparent',
          padding: 4,
          cursor: 'pointer'
        }}
      >
        <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
          <path d="M11 4a7 7 0 1 0 0 14 7 7 0 0 0 0-14z" stroke="currentColor" strokeWidth="2" strokeLinecap="round" strokeLinejoin="round"/>
          <path d="M21 21l-4.35-4.35" stroke="currentColor" strokeWidth="2" strokeLinecap="round" strokeLinejoin="round"/>
        </svg>
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

Notes and accessibility:
- Use a <button> for the icon rather than a div so it’s keyboard-focusable and accessible.
- Provide aria-label describing the action.
- Trim the input value and check for empty before calling the search function if you only want to call on non-empty input.
- Adjust padding-right on the input to match icon width + spacing.

If you want, tell me which framework (plain, React, Vue, Angular) or exact styling you prefer and I can tailor the example further.

